    <statement><p>Convert the following point from rectangular to cylindrical coordinates:
<me>(4\sqrt{2},-4\sqrt{2},-7).</me></p>
<p><m>(r,\theta,z) =</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert> Usage: </alert>To enter a point, for example <m>(x,y,z)</m>, type "(x, y, z)".</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(8,\frac{7\pi }{4},-7\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>(x,y,z)=(4\sqrt{2},-4\sqrt{2},-7)</m>. We first find <m>r</m>:
<me>r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=\sqrt{(4\sqrt{2})^2+(-4\sqrt{2})^2}=8.</me>
Since the point <m>(x,y)=(4\sqrt{2},-4\sqrt{2})</m> lies in the fourth quadrant, <m>\frac{3\pi}{2}\leq \theta\leq 2\pi</m>. Hence
<me>\tan \theta=\frac{y}{x}=\frac{-4\sqrt{2}}{4\sqrt{2}}=-1,</me>
so <m>\theta=\frac{7 \pi}{4}</m>.



The cylindrical coordinates are thus
<me>(r,\theta,z)=\left( 8, \frac{7 \pi}{4}, -7 \right).</me>


</solution>
